; Network-effect boundary for one explicitly selected C++ declaration.
;
; This is the shipped specialization. Its ship-time declaration selection is
; the empty set, so a repository that enables this policy without editing the
; selection block below carries no purity obligation at all. Purity is never
; inferred from a function name, a namespace spelling, a file name, a comment,
; or an attribute.
;
; Activation
; ----------
; 1. Ship or install the reviewed C++ semantic model that declares the concrete
;    network API identity with the effect `bifrost.network_io`.
;    `semantic-packs/web-network/cpp/bifrost.web-network-cpp.json` is that
;    reviewed model. Place it under `.bifrost/semantic-models/`, where a C++
;    workspace activates it without further configuration.
; 2. Replace both declaration selections in the analysis block below with the
;    exact declaration(s) this repository protects. A member function is a
;    `method` qualified by its class; a namespace-scope function is a
;    `function`:
;      (language cpp
;        (enclosing-decl
;          (inside-decl (class :name "PriceCalculator")
;            (method :name "total"))))
;      (language cpp (enclosing-decl (function :name "total")))
;    A member defined out of line (`long PriceCalculator::total() {...}`)
;    matches its class through `inside-decl` from the Bifrost engine change
;    in BrokkAi/bifrost-dev#5942 on. An engine without that change selects
;    nothing for the qualified form and reports the run as an empty
;    selection.
; 3. Enable the policy: `bifrost --policy-id
;    bifrost.effects.cpp.selected-boundary-no-network-io --fail-on error`.
;
; Why this API
; ------------
; The reviewed identity is Boost.Asio's namespace-scope function template
; `boost::asio::connect(socket, endpoints)` at its written arity two, declared
; in <boost/asio/connect.hpp>. A call written with the qualified name is C++
; qualified lookup in namespace `boost::asio`, which argument-dependent lookup
; does not extend. When no workspace declaration answers the call, it publishes
; the external identity `boost.asio.connect` from that qualified name. A
; workspace declaration of the same qualified name, or a workspace namespace
; the enclosing scope finds first, such as `shop::boost::asio`, wins lookup and
; does not bind the reviewed identity. Because the identity comes from the
; qualified name rather than a resolved header declaration, a namespace alias
; or an unindexed redeclaration that renames `boost::asio::connect` would still
; bind it (BrokkAi/bifrost-dev#5943).
;
; POSIX `connect` and libcurl `curl_easy_perform` are file-scope functions. A
; bare call to one of them from a C++ translation unit stays an open boundary,
; because argument-dependent lookup can add a same-named function from the
; namespace of any argument's type, which the analyzer cannot rule out for an
; external argument type. Those identities belong to the C row, where C has no
; such lookup.
;
; Member calls in the workspace chain bind when the callee is a non-virtual
; member of a class with no base clause. A virtual member, or a member of a
; class that writes a base clause, keeps the dispatch open until the analyzer
; publishes structured virtual metadata (BrokkAi/bifrost-dev#3510), so the run
; publishes an unmet obligation rather than a clean verdict.
;
; The rule body is shared across languages; only the language label, the
; activated model, and this selection block differ.
;
; The join
; --------
; `procedure-effects` projects one row per (procedure, effect id) with the
; classification (direct or transitive), certainty, timing, coverage and a
; bounded witness chain. Rows are keyed on the declaration domain's own
; `procedure_id`, so the join to the selected relation is declaration-identity
; equality.
;
; The absence claim
; -----------------
; `(exactly 0)` is an absence claim, so it is conclusive only when the effect
; relation's coverage is exhaustive. A selected declaration that reaches an
; unresolved helper leaves the effect set open; the run then publishes an unmet
; obligation instead of reporting a clean verdict, and a helper chain the
; analyzer cannot expand is never reported as an absence.
(policy
  :schema-version 1
  :id "bifrost.effects.cpp.selected-boundary-no-network-io"
  :name "Selected C++ boundary reaches no network effect"
  :message "the selected C++ boundary reaches the declared network effect"
  :severity error
  :description "One explicitly selected C++ declaration must not reach a declared network effect, directly or through workspace helpers. The effect is declared by the reviewed C++ semantic model on the exact external API identity, never inferred from a function name, a namespace spelling, a file name, a comment, or an attribute. The ship-time selection is empty; configure the declaration-selection block before enabling this policy."
  :help-uri "https://bifrost.brokk.ai/static-analysis-policies/#network-effect-boundary"
  :tags ["effects" "network" "boundary" "cpp"]
  :analysis
    (analysis
      :type assertion
      ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
      ; Declaration-selection block. Ship-time selection: the empty set.
      ; Replace both `(except ...)` expressions below with the exact
      ; declaration this repository protects. The `effect` binding repeats the
      ; same selection on purpose: `procedure-effects` derives a closure for
      ; every declaration its input query returns, so repeating the selection
      ; is what keeps the policy from deriving effects for declarations it did
      ; not select. With the ship-time empty selection it derives none, the
      ; assertion has no group to judge, and the run is vacuously clean.
      ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
      (bind :name selected
        :query (rql :schema-version 1
          (language cpp
            (except
              (enclosing-decl (function))
              (enclosing-decl (function))))))
      (bind :name effect
        :query (rql :schema-version 1
          (language cpp
            (procedure-effects
              (except
                (enclosing-decl (function))
                (enclosing-decl (function)))))))
      (join :left selected :right effect :on ((id procedure_id)))
      (group :name selected-boundary :by (selected.id)
        (aggregate :name network-effects :op count
          :where ((effect.effect_id eq "bifrost.network_io")
                  (effect.derivation eq declared))))
      (assert :group selected-boundary :value network-effects :cardinality (exactly 0))))
